  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08865v1</id>
    <title>Rethinking Agent Trajectory Compression: A Workflow-Aware Perspective</title>
    <updated>2026-11-06T01:47:55Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08865v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.08865v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>As long-horizon agents increasingly rely on multi-step tool interactions to accomplish complex tasks, their execution trajectories continuously accumulate structured outputs and intermediate artifacts, leading to growing context overhead. Existing compression methods primarily rely on text importance or fixed truncation rules, often overlooking cross-step dependencies within workflows and potentially discarding critical information required for subsequent task execution. To address this challenge, we propose WAAC, a workflow-aware framework for agent trajectory compression. WAAC structurally models successful historical trajectories to extract reusable workflow compression experience and incorporates online backward dependency analysis to dynamically identify information retention requirements. By combining current dependency constraints with historical experience, WAAC selects appropriate compression strategies for different types of historical information. Experiments on PinchBench and Odysseys demonstrate that WAAC achieves an overall score of 87.6 and a perfect completion rate of 19.0%, respectively, while reducing execution costs by 19.7% and 29.1% compared with the strongest baseline methods. These results demonstrate the effectiveness of workflow-aware trajectory compression in improving task performance and cost efficiency for long-horizon agents. Code and resources are available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/waac-6527/.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-06T01:47:55Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>12 pages, 4 figures</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Qijia Zhuang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Rui Liu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yuxiang Ren</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08763v1</id>
    <title>CrowdDirector: Runtime Orchestration of Crowd Simulation through Natural-Language Directives</title>
    <updated>2026-11-05T22:18:10Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08763v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.08763v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Natural language offers an intuitive way to direct a virtual crowd while a simulation is running. Handwritten rules execute efficiently but cannot respond flexibly to varied instructions, whereas asking a large language model (LLM) to control the crowd at every update introduces repeated latency, cost, and the risk of unsupported actions. We present CrowdDirector, a framework that uses an LLM only when the user adds or changes an instruction. The LLM translates the request into a structured instruction, and a scene validator checks whether the entities and actions it references exist in the current Unity scene. Accepted instructions are stored and remain continuously active as the simulation is running. At each simulation tick, a graph neural network uses the current scene state and active instruction to choose an available action and target for each agent, while Unity handles movement, interactions, and animation. The network is trained offline on simulated scenarios, with LLM guidance on how agents should respond to instructions. Our evaluations show the scene validator's feasibility decisions matched human judgements more often than the LLM's own assessment, and on a behavioural benchmark CrowdDirector follows user instructions more closely than handwritten rules while avoiding repeated LLM calls. It therefore provides a practical middle ground between fixed rules and continuous LLM control.</summary>
    <category term="cs.GR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.MA" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-05T22:18:10Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.GR"/>
    <author>
      <name>Xiaohan Sun</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Vuk Radovanovic</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Merce Alvarez</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Carol O'Sullivan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yiorgos Chrysanthou</name>
    </author>
